From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 13:59:44 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] canbus: Reject unrepresentable bitrates
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The hardware timing calculations divided the peripheral clock by CANBUS_FREQUENCY before checking candidate quanta.  A non-divisible request silently discarded that remainder, and several backends encoded fall-through or default fields when no legal timing tuple existed.  The firmware still exported the requested frequency, hiding that the node sampled a different physical bit period from clients which trusted it.

Require exact peripheral-clock division and controller-specific prescaler limits in bxCAN, M_CAN/FDCAN, and LPC timing searches.  Validate can2040’s 16.8 PIO divider in the same way, including its range.  Return an explicit invalid sentinel instead of ever programming an approximate result.

Board initialization runs before sched_main establishes shutdown’s longjmp recovery point, so calling shutdown directly from can_init would itself be unsafe.  Publish an invalid timing result to the existing canserial receive task and wake it; the driver returns before programming or starting CAN, and that first normal task pass reports a controlled Invalid CAN bitrate shutdown.

All previously representable settings retain identical timing fields and wire behavior.  Only configurations which could not produce their advertised bitrate are refused.

Signed-off-by: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>

diff --git a/src/atsam/fdcan.c b/src/atsam/fdcan.c
index 9580e221..fbd4770a 100644
--- a/src/atsam/fdcan.c
+++ b/src/atsam/fdcan.c
@@ -331,6 +331,17 @@ compute_btr(uint32_t pclock, uint32_t bitrate)
         Use the lowest brp where ts1 and ts2 are in valid range
      */
 
+    /*
+     * Integer division here used to discard any pclock remainder.  The NBTP
+     * value then described the nearest lower number of peripheral clocks while
+     * CANBUS_FREQUENCY still advertised the requested rate to the host.  CAN
+     * nodes need the same nominal bit period to arbitrate reliably, so silently
+     * selecting a nearby rate is worse than refusing an invalid build-time
+     * choice.  Require an exact peripheral-clock division and a legal 1..512
+     * prescaler before programming the controller.
+     */
+    if (!bitrate || pclock % bitrate)
+        return CAN_INVALID_BTR;
     uint32_t bit_clocks = pclock / bitrate; // clock ticks per bit
 
     uint32_t sjw = 2;
@@ -338,15 +349,16 @@ compute_btr(uint32_t pclock, uint32_t bitrate)
     // Find number of time quantas that gives us the exact wanted bit time
     for (qs = 18; qs > 9; qs--) {
         // check that bit_clocks / quantas is an integer
-        uint32_t brp_rem = bit_clocks % qs;
-        if (brp_rem == 0)
-            break;
+        if (bit_clocks % qs)
+            continue;
+        uint32_t brp = bit_clocks / qs;
+        if (!brp || brp > 512)
+            continue;
+        uint32_t time_seg2 = qs / 8; // sample at ~87.5%
+        uint32_t time_seg1 = qs - (1 + time_seg2);
+        return make_btr(sjw, time_seg1, time_seg2, brp);
     }
-    uint32_t brp       = bit_clocks / qs;
-    uint32_t time_seg2 = qs / 8; // sample at ~87.5%
-    uint32_t time_seg1 = qs - (1 + time_seg2);
-
-    return make_btr(sjw, time_seg1, time_seg2, brp);
+    return CAN_INVALID_BTR;
 }
 
 void
@@ -373,6 +385,10 @@ can_init(void)
     uint32_t pclock = get_pclock_frequency(CANx_GCLK_ID);
 
     uint32_t btr = compute_btr(pclock, CONFIG_CANBUS_FREQUENCY);
+    if (btr == CAN_INVALID_BTR) {
+        canserial_set_invalid_bitrate();
+        return;
+    }
 
     /*##-1- Configure the CAN #######################################*/
 
